.TH tapectl-cartridge-retire 1  "tapectl-cartridge-retire " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-cartridge\-retire \- Retire a cartridge permanently — it must never be written again
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl cartridge retire\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-reason\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIBARCODE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Retire a cartridge permanently — it must never be written again
.PP
ADR\-0011: for wear, read errors, or any judgement that the medium is no longer fit to hold data. This is NOT an erasure and NOT a location change: the bytes may still be readable, but nothing will ever write to this cartridge again, and `volume init` refuses to bind it. `cartridge unretire` is the way back — the operator saying they were wrong about the medium; `cartridge mark\-erased` is a different statement, that the bytes are gone (ADR\-0011, corrected 2026\-09\-14).
.PP
ADR\-0008 Tier 2: the coverage impact is displayed first, and `\-\-force`/`\-\-yes` is required when a unit is left below its policy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<REASON>\fR
Why (appended to the cartridge\*(Aqs notes, never overwriting them)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Waive the ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 prompt: proceed when the retirement leaves a live version below its policy but above zero. It does NOT defeat the Tier\-3 refusal (issue #147) — a cartridge holding the last eligible copy of a live version is refused outright and no flag reaches it. See cli::consent
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept (issue #147)
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, receipts. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home (issue #109).
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, receipts, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME (issue #109).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIBARCODE\fR>
